
Andrew: Ah, you're here!

Player: Dr. Mundo sent me.

Andrew: He told me about your investigation.

Andrew: You've learned that many atoms tend to become more stable when they have a full outer electron shell.

Player: That's the Octet Rule, right?

Andrew: Exactly! But knowing the rule is only the beginning.

Andrew: Atoms can gain, lose, or share electrons. These interactions can lead to chemical bonds.

Player: Is that what we're going to practice?

Andrew: Yes! I want to introduce you to Fusion.

Player: What does Fusion do?

Andrew: In Atomix, Fusion allows compatible Atomons to combine into another Atomon.

Andrew: Think of it as a gameplay activity that helps us explore how different atoms can form combinations.

Player: So Fusion is the same thing as chemical bonding?

Andrew: Not exactly. Fusion in our world is a game mechanic, while chemical bonding describes how atoms interact through their electrons.

Andrew: But learning about electron arrangements and bonding will help you understand why combinations of atoms are possible.

Andrew: Before you try Fusion yourself, we'll begin with a short training battle.

Andrew: Pay attention to the Battle UI. I'll guide you through the important parts.

Player: I'm ready!

Andrew: Good! Let's begin.

